  "codex-passive-usage-attribution" [label="Complete passive\naccounting and\nongoing-work\nresource awareness\nREADY", shape=box, fillcolor="#fff7dc", color="#aa8a31", tooltip="Technical assessment/repairs and bounded delivery checks returned; Jörn graded this attempt FAIL. Earlier root/supervisor completion claims do not establish human acceptance of the workflow. Retained evidence: owned-response/fork/counter repairs,20 focused native-reader tests, selected-root projection compatibility, fresh-agent command/interpretation discovery, three root/worker usage-note receipts, and actual time/context-tool delivery. C hook remains withdrawn and inactive. Repeated objective drift, rejected overbuilding, scope takeover and premature endings remain session failures; useful component checks do not cancel that verdict. At closure Jörn questioned giving up and explicitly requested notifying Fix Codex thread orchestration. Root’s earlier ending was its own choice, not an explicit user cancellation. The requested notification was accepted through native tool-output transport by existing orchestration thread01a103dc-762f-7ef1-a094-4b0247b290ff; receipt not yet verified.\nplan_gap: Workflow acceptance failed for this attempt. No further root execution or invented approval question selected at closure. Permanent/all-agent coverage, native export deduplication, active-work/task joins and quota/billing conversion remain unestablished. Technical artifacts are retained without a PASS/readiness claim.\nproject: workflows\nauthorization: Jörn requested checking available scripts and applicable other-session ownership for passive resource attribution. Bounded read-only observation and correction of an attribution grouping defect; no quota experiment, new service or budget enforcement selected. Follow-up bounded readiness review covers discoverability, automation, session-log attribution, trust and fork behavior; it does not activate a new service, hook, skill, goal or quota experiment. J